The main difficulty is that a node can be removed even when it is the head of the linked list.

To handle this cleanly, we create a dummy node before head:

dummy → 1 → 2 → 6 → 3 → 4 → 5 → 6
↑
head

Now every node, including the original head, has a previous node. This means we can use the same deletion logic throughout the list.

We maintain two pointers:

prev → node before the current node
curr → node currently being checked

When curr->val == val, simply skip curr:

prev → curr → next

becomes

prev ─────────→ next

If the value doesn't match, move both pointers forward.

Approach
Create a dummy node pointing to head.
Set prev = dummy and curr = head.
Traverse the list using curr.
If curr->val == val:
Remove it using prev->next = curr->next.
Do not move prev, because another matching node could immediately follow.
Otherwise:
Move prev to curr.
Move curr to the next node.
Return dummy->next as the new head.
🔑 Key idea
if (curr->val == val)
prev->next = curr->next;
else
prev = curr;

curr = curr->next;

Complexity
Time complexity:O(n) — we visit every node once.
Space complexity:O(1) — only a few pointers are used.
